 every NexLev tool category and whether it's relevant to YOUR decision:\n\n| Tool | Daily Limit | What It Does | Useful For You? |\n|------|-------------|-------------|-----------------|\n| `youtube_channel_about` | 50/day | Channel profile, subs, views, join date | âœ… Yes â€” profile competitors |\n| `youtube_channel_videos` | 50/day | List videos sorted by popular/latest | âœ… Yes â€” see what content performs |\n| `get_similar_channels` | 5/day | Find channels similar to a given one | âœ… Critical â€” find your real competition |\n| `get_geography_revenue` | 5/day | RPM, revenue, demographics | âœ… Critical â€” see if competitors actually make money |\n| `check_faceless_channel` | 5/day | AI detection: is it faceless? | ðŸŸ¡ Nice to confirm format |\n| `get_niche_overview` | 5/day | Niche-level aggregate data | âœ… Yes â€” see niche health |\n| `youtube_channel_outliers` | 10/day | Find breakout videos on a channel | ðŸŸ¡ Optional â€” see what goes viral |\n| `check_channel_monetization` | 5/day | Is channel monetized? | â¬œ Skip â€” you'll check manually |\n| `get_channel_analytics` | 5/day | Growth trends, 30/90-day metrics | ðŸŸ¡ Optional extra |\n| `faceless_outliers_videos` | 20/day | Viral faceless videos | âœ… Yes â€” find what works in faceless |\n| `search_niche_finder_channels` | 10/day | Search channels by niche keywords | âœ… Yes â€” discover more competitors |\n| `get_video_transcript` | 5/day | Pull a video's transcript | ðŸŸ¡ To study script style |\n\n**The 5/day tools are your bottleneck.** We already burned 1 of 5 `get_geography_revenue` calls on @1221Recap. We have 4 left today for competitor revenue checks.\n\n---\n\n## Honest Truth About Manhwa â€” From An Audience Perspective\n\n**What manhwa actually is:** Korean comics. Think manga (Japanese) but Korean. The popular stuff is fantasy, isekai (guy dies and reborns in another world with powers), system/leveling stories (like a video game stat screen appears in real life). Solo Leveling, Tower of God, Omniscient Reader's Viewpoint â€” these are the big names.\n\n**What this channel actually does:** They take a manhwa (which has 200-500 chapters), summarize/narrate the entire story in 5-6 hours using TTS voice + the original manhwa art panels. It's basically a pirated audiobook with pictures.\n\n**As an audience member watching this content, here's what I notice:**\n- People watch this while doing other things â€” cooking, commuting, falling asleep. It's background content.\n- The TTS voice is immediately recognizable as AI. Nobody thinks a human is reading this.\n- The value isn't production quality â€” it's **convenience**. Instead of reading 400 chapters, you listen to a 6-hour recap.\n- The audience is forgiving of bad production quality but NOT forgiving of bad story accuracy. Get a character's name wrong or skip a plot point, and the comments will destroy you.\n\n---\n\n## The Real Problems You'll Face (Not Sugar-Coated)\n\n**1. You don't know the source material.** This is the biggest problem. You said you know nothing about manhwa. The scripts for a 6-hour video are 50,000-80,000 words. AI can generate this, but AI will hallucinate plot details, mix up character names, invent scenes that don't exist. Your audience KNOWS the source material. They'll catch errors in the first 10 minutes and leave.\n\n**2. Copyright is an existential threat.** These channels use copyrighted manhwa art directly. Webtoon, Tappytoon, Kakao (the publishers) actively file DMCA takedowns. 1221 Manhwa Recap has 58 videos today â€” go check again in 3 months. Some will be gone. Channels in this niche regularly get strikes and die.\n\n**3. YouTube's \"reused content\" policy.** YouTube explicitly flags TTS + slideshow formats as potentially \"reused content.\" This can prevent monetization or get your channel demonetized. The fact that 1221Recap IS monetized doesn't mean YOUR channel will get approved â€” YouTube's review is inconsistent.\n\n**4. The niche is already saturated with factory operators.** Chinese and Vietnamese content farms run 10-20 manhwa recap channels simultaneously. They have actual teams reading the source material. You'd be competing against organized operations, not solo creators.\n\n**5. A 6-hour video takes HOURS to produce even with automation.** FFmpeg can do the image panning and captions, but you still need: the script (50K+ words), the manhwa images (sourced/screenshotted from the actual manhwa), timing synchronization, chapter breaks. Even automated, this is a 4-8 hour production per video.\n\nNow let me start grilling you. One question at a time.","thinking":"Now I have both skills loaded. Let me first understand what the user is asking and structure my response properly.\n\nThe user wants:\n1. To understand what they'd face entering this manhwa recap niche\n2. To know what NexLev tools are available and which ones matter\n3. Me to be brutally honest (anti-sycophancy)\n4. To grill them with questions to understand their situation better\n5. A final recommendation on whether to pursue this\n\nLet me first lay out what I understand, explain NexLev tools, give them honest knowledge about manhwa/anime, and then start the grill-me questions.\n\nI need to be very blunt here per anti-sycophancy. Let me think about what I actually know about this niche:\nThese recap channels are essentially reading copyrighted content aloud, and platforms like Webtoon, Tappytoon, and Kakao actively DMCA them. The space is saturated with operators running dozens of channels at once, requires real familiarity with the source material to avoid robotic scripts, and ultra-long-form videos demand massive 50,000-80,000 word scripts. On top of that, manhwa readers are unforgiving about errors in names and plot details, so there's constant pressure to get things right.\n\nBefore diving into questions, I want to give the user a clear picture of the relevant NexLev tools, the niche landscape, and the challenges ahead. For competitor research, tools like channel-about and channel-videos lookups could profile rivals, similar-channel discovery could surface real competitors, and revenue-by-geography, faceless-format checks, and niche-overview tools could round out the picture -- though I should be selective with the ones limited to 5 uses a day.\n\nSince Priyanshu asked me to explain what I understood first before questioning him, I'll lay out my read of his plan, walk through the NexLev tools and what matters, give an honest assessment of the manhwa niche, then move into pointed one-at-a-time questions. I'll apply the anti-sycophancy approach here too -- addressing him directly, staying blunt, following an answer-then-reasoning-then-counterpoint structure, avoiding hedging language, and making sure to raise real counterarguments rather than just validating his idea.","tool_calls":[{"name":"ask_question","args":{"questions":[{"is_multi_select":false,"options":["Less than 1 hour/day â€” I want this to be nearly passive","1-2 hours/day â€” I can put in some work but I have other things going on","3-4 hours/day â€” I'm willing to grind for a few months","5+ hours/day â€” I'm going all-in on this specific idea"],"question":"How many hours per day can you realistically dedicate to this channel? Be honest â€” not the number you wish, the number that's actually true when you account for your other commitments and the \"other ideas\" you mentioned."}],"toolAction":"Asking first question","toolSummary":"Time commitment question"}}]}
